in 1997 Princess Diana shocked the world  by walking through a minefield in Angola  a short walk that drew more attention to  landmines than any other public campaign  22 years later  Prince Harry was marking another  important step forward as the government  in Angola has pledged 47 million pounds  to clear the mines I had the privilege  in 2013 a visiting angola with the halo  Trust in cuando Kabongo in the far  southeast of what is a vast beautiful  country I saw a struggling community in  a deserted landscape unable to make use  of the land yet the potential to turn  this land into a sustainable source for  its people in fact I was told just the  other day of the positive transformation  in Colombo since my mother walked that  minefield all those years ago what is  less well known is the impact land mines  can have on conservation and wildlife  and therefore visits to Mozambique in  2010 and then Angola three years later  had a huge impact on hairy  in Angola they are slowly clearing the  thousands of mines left from the Civil  War but the knock-on impact has been  huge the fear has stopped farmers from  using their land and the precious  wildlife is being targeted by poachers  because of rising levels of poverty the  area still has land mines fields  scattered throughout its territory which  in spite of government effort in  partnership with the mining companies  still deny human access to part of the  vast resources they put to lives of  wildlife flora and the entire coast  systems at risk  Angola may be benefitting from this  latest investment but the halo trust  hope the Dukes involvement will wake up  the wider international community the  numbers of people being hurt every year  rising at a horrible rate so we've got  to get at this we've got to do something  about it it's a humanitarian crisis that  we've got to get to grips with and so  his influence means that we can get  countries once again to commit us the  British have 100 million pounds it's a  start but I'd like to see more  it is anticipated that Harry and Megan  may visit Angola later this year as part  of a royal tour continuing his mother's  groundbreaking work Rhiannon Mills Sky  News
 

